/**
@page vector_dispatch Vector dispatch and the capability ladder

@tableofcontents

LAME's hottest quantization loops carry hand-written SIMD implementations that
are chosen at run time from the instruction sets the executing CPU reports. This
page records the shape of that dispatch ladder and, in particular, the
decisions that are easy to second-guess later: why the ladder has no SSE4.1 or
SSE4.2 rung, why the variable-bitrate noise routine is SSE2-only with no AVX2
tier, and why the AVX-512 rung covers the quantization loop and not the table
search. All of them are counter-intuitive, so the reasoning is kept here rather
than only in the commit history &mdash; several because the code was written and
the answer still turned out to be no, or not yet, which is exactly the kind of
result that gets repeated if it is not written down.

@section vector_dispatch_ladder The ladder

@c vector_implementation() returns a @c vector_impl_t naming the tier this
instance runs. Every dispatch site tests <em>">= the tier my routine
needs"</em>, so a routine that exists only at a lower tier is still selected on a
machine that offers a higher one. There is exactly one selector and one
enumerator; adding a tier is one branch in the selector and one row in the table
in @c util.c that answers every question about vector routines.

The ladder is deliberately <b>scalar &rarr; SSE2 &rarr; AVX2 &rarr;
AVX-512</b>, with nothing between SSE2 and AVX2. On x86-64 the SSE2 tier needs
no feature probe at all: every x86-64 CPU has SSE2, as does every 32-bit x86 CPU
since 2001, so the SSE2 win is unconditional. The two rungs above it are gated
on run-time feature bits.

The lever is @c --vector, which names the set to run rather than the sets to
avoid: @c --vector @c none for the scalar code, @c --vector followed by a set's
name to pin an encode to it even where the processor offers a wider one. The
deprecated @c --noasm maps onto it and says so.

@subsection vector_dispatch_reach How a tier actually reaches a routine

Worth stating explicitly, because getting it wrong is invisible. Two different
routes carry the tier to a dispatch site, and only one of them follows
@c vector_implementation() automatically:

<ul>
<li><b>Straight from the instance.</b> @c quantize_xrpow() is handed
@c vector_implementation(gfc) at the call site, so a new tier is reached there as
soon as the selector can return it.</li>
<li><b>Through a function pointer.</b> The table search is not called directly:
@c huffman_init() installs one of @c choose_table_c / @c choose_table_sse2 /
@c choose_table_avx2 in @c gfc->choose_table, and <em>that wrapper</em> is what
passes a fixed @c vector_impl_t into @c choose_table_x(), which in turn hands it
to @c ix_max_v() and the counting routines. A tier with no wrapper of its own
therefore never reaches any of them, no matter what the processor reports or
what the user asked for.</li>
</ul>

The second route is why an AVX-512 tier can look complete &mdash; probe, table
row, enumerator, compiled kernels, exported symbols, passing unit tests &mdash;
and still never execute a single one of its table-search instructions inside an
encode. It also means a bitstream comparison between two tiers cannot tell you
the higher one ran: identical output is exactly what an unreachable kernel
produces. Any new rung wants a check that <em>fails</em> if its routines are
absent, not one that passes when nothing changes.

@section vector_dispatch_no_sse4 Why there is no SSE4.1 / SSE4.2 tier

For every routine vectorized here, an SSE4.1 or SSE4.2 implementation is no
faster than &mdash; and usually slower than &mdash; the SSE2 one. This inverts
the usual "a newer instruction set is faster" intuition, and the reason is
specific and measurable.

The largest win is the Huffman table search (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()):
find a region's maximum quantized coefficient and sum code lengths over table
lookups. Every value it touches is bounded by @c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206, which is
below 2<sup>15</sup> = 32768, so all of it fits in signed 16-bit lanes. That one
fact inverts the instruction-set ranking:

- <b>SSE2</b> has the packed 16-bit integer operations this needs &mdash;
  @c pmaxsw (max), @c pminsw (clamp), @c packssdw (pack two 32-bit vectors into
  one 16-bit vector) and @c pmaddwd (the lookup's index arithmetic in one
  instruction) &mdash; and processes <b>eight lanes per instruction</b>.
- <b>SSE4.1</b>'s relevant additions (@c pmaxsd, @c pminsd, @c pmulld,
  @c pextrd) work on 32-bit lanes: <b>four lanes per instruction</b>. For data
  that fits in 16 bits, that is half the width for no benefit.

Measured on a Core i7-10700 (GCC 16.1, ns per element at region length 576):
the region maximum is about a quarter faster in the 8-lane SSE2 form than in
SSE4.1's @c pmaxsd (0.058 vs 0.082 ns), and the table lookup's manual gather is
roughly 3.0&times; scalar under SSE2 versus only about 1.8&times; under SSE4.1.
SSE4.1 loses on both primitives.

SSE4.2 adds only string and CRC instructions (@c pcmpestri, @c pcmpistri,
@c crc32) over SSE4.1 &mdash; none of them a numeric SIMD operation an audio
encoder's inner loops can use &mdash; so for this work SSE4.2 is identical to
SSE4.1, i.e. "nothing over SSE2". This is not academic: the project's FreeBSD
validation host is a Westmere Xeon (SSE4.2, no AVX at all), and it receives the
entire table-search win because that win lives at the SSE2 baseline and needs
nothing above it.

The float routines (@c quantize_lines_xrpow(), @c calc_sfb_noise_x34()) do not
need SSE4.x either: SSE2 already has the full single-precision float instruction
set (@c mulps, @c addps, @c subps, @c cvttps2dq), and SSE4.1's float additions
(@c dpps, @c blendps, @c roundps, @c insertps) buy nothing here &mdash; there is
no dot product, no blend, and the truncation is @c cvttps (SSE2), not a
rounding-mode @c roundps.

@subsection vector_dispatch_saturation The 16-bit correctness constraint

@c packssdw saturates at 32767, so the 16-bit maximum is a drop-in for
@c ix_max() only <em>as @c choose_table() uses it</em>: the result is only ever
tested against @c IXMAX_VAL and a @c linbits bucket, and @c IXMAX_VAL @c = @c 8206
is far below the saturation point. Values that matter are therefore exact, and
anything larger clamps to 32767, which still lands in the "> @c IXMAX_VAL, give
up" branch. A compile-time assertion beside @c ix_max_sse2() (in @c takehiro.c)
fails the build if @c IXMAX_VAL is ever raised past the saturation point. The
trick is correct for this one caller, not in general.

@section vector_dispatch_no_vbr_avx2 Why calc_sfb_noise_x34() has no AVX2 tier

@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() estimates per-scalefactor-band quantization noise; it is
a variable-bitrate routine (the constant-bitrate path never calls it). An AVX2
version was written, measured, and dropped: on a Core i7-10700 it ran at
1.00&times; &plusmn; 0.01 over the SSE2 tier for a whole VBR encode on both GCC
and MSVC &mdash; inside the measurement noise, against a real 6&ndash;8% the same
AVX2 tier delivers on the constant-bitrate routines. Three reasons compound:

1. <b>Gather-bound.</b> Each four-element block does two data-dependent table
   lookups (@c adj43[] in the quantizer step, @c pow43[] in the noise step). AVX2
   would do them as one eight-wide @c vgatherdps, but on Skylake-family and older
   cores @c vgatherdps is microcoded &mdash; roughly one element loaded per cycle
   internally, i.e. the same scalar loads wearing one opcode. It removes the
   reassembly shuffles, which were never the cost.

2. <b>The reduction is pinned to a four-block.</b> To keep every CPU of an
   architecture bit-identical, the sum of squares is reduced as
   (x0&sup2;+x2&sup2;)+(x1&sup2;+x3&sup2;) per four elements into a running
   scalar. An AVX2 tier processing eight at a time must still reduce its two
   halves separately, in that order &mdash; it cannot carry an eight-wide
   accumulator without reassociating the sum and moving the output per-CPU. So
   width helps only the cheap multiply / subtract / square around the two
   expensive gathers.

3. <b>Amdahl, twice.</b> The SSE2 vectorization of this routine already moves a
   whole VBR encode by only about 6% on MSVC and about 0% on GCC (whose compiler
   auto-vectorizes the scalar loop competently), because VBR spends most of its
   time in the psychoacoustic model, not here. AVX2's increment is a fraction of
   that already-small share.

The capability ladder explicitly permits a routine to live at one tier only.
@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() is the converse of an AVX2-only routine: an SSE2 routine
with no AVX2 version, because AVX2 has nothing to offer it. Carrying a second
tier that cannot be shown to do anything is pure cost &mdash; a translation unit,
a build entry, a dispatch branch and a test path &mdash; so it is not carried.

@section vector_dispatch_no_avx512 The AVX-512 tier, and why it stops at constant bitrate

An AVX-512 tier above AVX2 was written in two stages, taken to hardware that has
the instruction set, and measured end to end on three machines from two vendors.
The constant-bitrate stage is in the ladder. The variable-bitrate stage is not,
and was deleted: it failed a bit-exactness test against the tier below it *and*
it was slower than that tier on the very mode it was written for.

The SSE4.x decision above rests on microbenchmarks and an instruction-count
argument. This one rests on timed encodes of the finished code, taken by three
members of the lame-dev list on machines the project does not own, because no
machine here has the instruction set.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_built What was built

Two stages, separate because they carried different risks:

- <b>Stage 1</b> &mdash; the run-time rung, plus AVX-512 forms of the Huffman
  table search (@c ix_max(), @c choose_table()) and the quantization loop
  (@c quantize_lines_xrpow()). These are elementwise, or reduce with an
  order-independent maximum, so widening them cannot move the output. This is
  the stage where the extra width was free of any correctness tax.
- <b>Stage 2</b> &mdash; @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(), in two reduction orders: one
  reproducing the narrower tier's pinned per-four-block association exactly, and
  an opt-in freely-reducing form built only to price what that pinning costs.

Only stage 1 survives, and the split is visible in the ladder itself: the two
rungs are not equivalent in coverage. @c quantize_lines_xrpow() is reached only
from @c count_bits(), which only @c quantize.c calls, so that kernel is
constant-bitrate by construction; @c choose_table() is reached from
@c noquant_count_bits() as well and therefore runs on both paths. Stage 2's
kernel was the one piece of AVX-512 that a variable-bitrate encode would have
spent real time in, and it is the piece that was dropped.

While the question was open the tier was confined to alpha builds. That gate is
gone: the measurement settled it, so the tier is now built wherever the compiler
can build it and selected wherever the processor can run it, exactly like SSE2
and AVX2, with no configure switch and no version test.

No machine in the project has AVX-512 and QEMU's TCG does not emulate it, so
none of it could be executed here. It was compiled by four toolchains,
disassembled and read instruction by instruction, and covered by unit tests
&mdash; and then sent to lame-dev as a test kit, because what was left genuinely
needed the hardware.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_tested What was tested, and where

Three machines ran the kit, on Linux, encoding 16-bit stereo 44.1&nbsp;kHz
music &mdash; between them the first execution of these instructions anywhere:

| CPU | microarchitecture | compiler | what it ran |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| AMD Ryzen 9 7950X3D | Zen 4 | GCC 13.3 | both stages, tests and timings |
| Intel Core i5-1035G1 | Ice Lake, client | GCC 14.2 | both stages, tests and timings, then a controlled re-run under a harness |
| AMD Ryzen 9 9950X3D | Zen 5 | GCC 15 | stage 1, timings only, under a harness |

- <b>Unit tests, stage 1: pass</b> on both machines that ran them. The kernels
  agree with the scalar reference across every region length, threshold and
  clamp boundary, index extreme and odd-length tail.
- <b>Encoder output, stage 1: bit-identical</b> on both machines that ran it, at
  @c -V2 and at @c -b @c 320. A full encode with the tier active matched the
  same build pinned to the tier below it, which is the check that covers the seams
  unit tests do not &mdash; notably @c count_bit_ESC()'s clamped-count
  accounting. Stage 1 is correct.
- <b>Unit tests, stage 2: the same single failure</b> on both, across two
  vendors and two compilers. @c calc_sfb_noise_x34() does not reproduce the
  narrower tier's result exactly. This is the routine whose sum chooses a
  scalefactor, so its test asserts bit-exactness against that tier rather than a
  tolerance, and the reduction is where the two part company: the pinned
  per-four-block association did not survive widening to sixteen lanes. A tier
  that cannot reproduce the existing output is not a candidate, independently of
  its speed.

Stage 2's <em>encoder</em> output was bit-identical anyway, on both machines.
That belongs here as a caution rather than as reassurance. The unit test drives
the routine across inputs an encode does not happen to produce, so it is the
more sensitive instrument; identical output on one clip is not evidence that two
tiers agree, and the exact-match test is the one to believe. Its companion test
&mdash; the one that asserts the harness <em>can</em> report a disagreement
&mdash; passed on both machines, so the failure is the code and not the
scaffolding.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_speed The measurement

A whole encode after a warm-up, comparing the tier against the AVX2 tier
selected explicitly &mdash; @c --vector @c avx2 against @c --vector @c avx512,
which is what that option exists for.

| CPU | stage | @c -V2 | @c -b @c 320 | runs per arm |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | ---: |
| Zen 4 7950X3D | 1 | wash | <b>1.0% slower</b> | 3 |
| Zen 5 9950X3D | 1 | <b>neutral</b> | <b>no difference</b> | 10 / 25 |
| Ice Lake i5-1035G1 | 1 | wash | <b>7.6% faster</b> | 3 |
| Ice Lake i5-1035G1 | 2 | <b>6.6% slower</b> | <b>6.5% faster</b> | 25 &times; 2 orderings |

The last row is the one that decided both halves of this, and it is worth being
precise about why it carries more than the others. It was taken under a
benchmarking harness reporting a spread, 25 runs per arm, on 16-bit stereo
44.1&nbsp;kHz music, on mains power &mdash; and then <em>repeated with the two
arms in the opposite order</em>. The same arm measured in both positions drifts
between 0.00% and 0.25%, so the ordering of the arms is excluded and that
machine's noise floor under a harness is a quarter of a percent. All four
comparisons separate their means by 24 to 40 standard errors.

Two results follow, in opposite directions:

- <b>Constant bitrate: the tier wins, ~6.5% at 320&nbsp;kbit/s.</b> The row is
  stage 2, but the number is a stage-1 number: stage 2's only kernel is
  @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(), reachable solely from inside @c vbrquantize.c, so it
  cannot execute in a constant-bitrate encode. The two builds run the same
  instructions here. The earlier three-run figure on the same machine, 7.6%,
  agrees in sign and roughly in size.
- <b>Variable bitrate: stage 2 loses, ~6.6% at @c -V2.</b> Nobody had measured
  variable bitrate under a harness before, on any machine; every "wash" above
  is three sequential runs. The regression belongs to stage 2's kernel rather
  than to stage 1: Zen 5 ran stage 1 alone under a harness and returned
  1.004&nbsp;s against 1.006&nbsp;s at @c -V2, and stage 2's kernel is the
  variable-bitrate inner loop while stage 1's shared @c choose_table() is three
  calls per granule.

The two AMD numbers are unchanged by all of this. On Zen 4 the arms do not
overlap &mdash; every AVX-512 run slower than every AVX2 run, reproduced on a
second input file, so it is not a property of the clip. On Zen 5 the harness
returned 1.655&nbsp;s against 1.655&nbsp;s at &sigma;&nbsp;0.005&nbsp;s: not
close, equal.

So the ladder gains a rung that pays on one microarchitecture, does nothing on
one, and costs about 1% on a third. It is enabled on all of them. The dispatch
ladder tests what the processor can execute and nothing else &mdash; no vendor
and no model enters the decision &mdash; and a percent at one setting on one
microarchitecture does not buy back the cost of a dispatcher whose behaviour
cannot be predicted from the hardware alone.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_why Why the width buys so little, and where it buys anything

The obvious explanation for the AMD numbers is the datapath: Zen 4 executes
512-bit operations as two 256-bit halves, so a sixteen-wide instruction does no
more per cycle than the eight-wide one it replaces. That is true of Zen 4, and it
is not the reason. Zen 5 is not narrower &mdash; its desktop parts are reported
to have widened that path &mdash; and Zen 5 measured at zero, not at some
fraction of a win. If width were the binding constraint, relieving it would have
shown something.

What remains is a property of the loops rather than of any CPU:

1. <b>The same two constraints as the AVX2 analysis above, unchanged.</b> The
   table-search kernels are bounded by @c IXMAX_VAL and already run eight 16-bit
   lanes at the SSE2 baseline; the band-noise kernel is gather-bound and its
   reduction is pinned to the narrower tier's association. Doubling the register
   width relieves none of that. Stage 2 is the demonstration: unpinning the
   reduction is what widening actually requires, and doing so cost the tier its
   correctness.

2. <b>Amdahl.</b> What was left to win after AVX2 is a small share of the encode,
   and a fraction of a small share is not measurable against run-to-run spread.

Zen 4's halved datapath is then a third-order detail. It explains why Zen 4
landed slightly on the wrong side of zero rather than exactly on it.

None of that explains the Ice Lake gain, and the honest position is that the
mechanism there is not established. The plausible reading is that a narrow-issue
mobile core benefits from halving the instruction count even where the datapath
is not the constraint, while Zen 5's much wider front end gets nothing from
fewer uops. That is consistent with every number above &mdash; which is exactly
what the datapath story was, for three days, before Zen 5 refuted it. Treat it
as a hypothesis. What would discriminate is per-kernel counter data on an Ice
Lake part showing whether the gain tracks uop count rather than memory
behaviour; nothing has produced that yet.

@subsection vector_dispatch_avx512_open What is still open

Two things, neither of them blocking.

<b>The mechanism of the Intel gain</b>, above. It matters less for whether to
carry the tier than for predicting the next microarchitecture, and it is the sort
of question that gets answered by hardware counters rather than by more encodes.

<b>Stage 1 at @c -V2 on Intel.</b> The one variable-bitrate figure taken under a
harness on that machine was a stage-2 build, so it prices the rejected kernel
rather than the shipped one. The evidence that stage 1 is neutral there is Zen
5's harness measurement, three sequential-run washes, and the call-frequency
argument &mdash; good enough to ship, not good enough to state as measured.

One note on scope, because it bounds how much any of this matters. Intel's client
parts have carried AVX-512 only within a window &mdash; Ice Lake through Tiger
Lake and Rocket Lake, withdrawn from Alder Lake onwards, and not returning until
AVX10 &mdash; so the machine that produced the gain is from that window rather
than representative of current client hardware. Zen 4 and Zen 5 are most of the
population this rung reaches, and on them it is worth nothing or costs a percent.
The rung is carried because it is free to carry, not because it is expected to
matter often.

@section vector_dispatch_consequence Consequence

There is no @c VECTOR_IMPL_SSE41 / @c VECTOR_IMPL_SSE42 enumerator, no configure
probe and no dispatch branch for them. Every x86-64 CPU, and every 32-bit x86 CPU
since 2001, receives the full table-search and quantization win at the SSE2
baseline the build already guarantees; AVX2 adds a further 6&ndash;8% on constant
bitrate and nothing measurable on variable bitrate.

There <em>is</em> a @c VECTOR_IMPL_AVX512 enumerator, and it is the top of the
ladder &mdash; but a partial rung. What it carries in an ordinary build is the
constant-bitrate <b>quantization loop</b>, where a controlled measurement on Ice
Lake gives about 6.5% at 320&nbsp;kbit/s, Zen 5 gives nothing and Zen 4 costs
about 1%. It does not carry @c calc_sfb_noise_x34(): that version was written,
and it both failed to reproduce the narrower tier's output exactly and ran 6.6%
slower at @c -V2 on the one machine where AVX-512 is worth anything at all.

@section vector_dispatch_avx512_experiment The AVX-512 table search, and why it is off

@c avx512_choose_table.c holds AVX-512 forms of @c ix_max() and the escape
counter. They compile, they are exported and they pass their unit tests. They do
not run, and that is on purpose for now.

There has never been a wrapper installing @c VECTOR_IMPL_AVX512 into
@c gfc->choose_table, so by the route described above the two routines were
unreachable from any encode &mdash; on every machine, at every setting, since
they were written. That is not a detail that changes a percentage: the field
measurements were taken on a build in which these routines could not execute, so
the number they produced belongs to the quantization loop alone, and nothing at
all is known about what the table-search kernels do to an encode's speed.

The wrapper now exists, and is installed only when
@c vector_avx512_choose_table_experiment() says so:

<ul>
<li>the build must be an <b>alpha</b>, so no release can take the path, and</li>
<li><b>@c LAME_AVX512_CHOOSE_TABLE</b> must be set in the environment to
something other than @c 0.</li>
</ul>

An alpha that has it on says so in the @c --verbose header
("<tt>experimental: AVX-512 Huffman table search enabled</tt>"), and only when
the processor really is running the AVX-512 tier &mdash; a tester who cannot see
the line did not measure the thing being asked about, which is the failure mode
this whole section exists to prevent.

The routines are compiled unconditionally rather than hidden behind a build
switch, so that the code being asked about is the code in the tree and a tester
needs no special build. When there are numbers this becomes either an ordinary
dispatch arm or a deletion; either way the experiment is one function in
@c util.c and its two callers, and @c LAME_AVX512_CHOOSE_TABLE finds all of it.

<b>It is not a constant-bitrate experiment.</b> The table search is reached
through @c noquant_count_bits(), which @c vbrquantize.c calls as well, so
switching this on changes which code a @c -V encode runs too. Both modes want
measuring.

That also puts a question mark next to a decision recorded above.
@c calc_sfb_noise_x34() was rejected for @c -V partly because its AVX-512 form
ran 6.6% slower than the narrower code. As a comparison that is sound &mdash;
both arms had the same table search, which in neither case executed &mdash; and
the other half of the rejection, that widening the sum is not bit-exact, does
not depend on speed at all and stands by itself. But the two are not
independent on real hardware: 512-bit instructions can pull the core clock
down, and a routine that is the only 512-bit code in an encode pays that on its
own, where one running alongside an already-512-bit table search does not. If
this experiment ever becomes the default, that measurement is worth taking again
before the number is quoted again &mdash; it was made in a @c -V encode where
nothing else was 512 bits wide.

@section vector_dispatch_gaps The shapes of the gaps

The ladder has three kinds of hole in it, for three different reasons, and
keeping them apart is the point of this page. SSE4 is missing because the case
was never made. AVX-512's variable-bitrate half is missing because the case was
made, built, and measured &mdash; and lost. AVX-512's table search is present but
switched off because the case has never actually been put: it was assumed to have
been, by everyone including its measurements.
*/
